1️⃣ How Lambda Expressions were introduced in Java:
-to make Java more modern,
 concise,
 and functional-programming friendly.
 - before java 8, java programming nedded more lengthy code to write even for small pieces of logic
 before java 8, (Anonymous class):

 Runnable r=new Runnable(){
 public void run(){
 sout("hello");
 }
 };

 with lambda Expression:
 Runnable r=()->System.out.println("hello");

 -So the goal was to make Java shorter, clearer, and easier to write.

 2️⃣ Reason Behind Introducing Lambda Expressions;
 1. Reduce Boilerplate Code
 -Earlier Java required writing a lot of unnecessary code.
 -Lambda allows writing behavior in a single line.
 example before java 8:
 Collection.sort(list,new Comparator<Integer>(){

 return a-b;
 }
 });

 After java 8:
 Collection.sort(list,(a,b)->a-b);

 2. Support Functional Programming
 Modern languages like:
 Scala
 Python
 JavaScript
 already supported functional programming.
 Java needed similar capabilities to stay modern.

 3. Enable Powerful APIs (like Streams)

 Lambda expressions enabled the Stream API in Java 8.
 list.stream()
     .filter(x->x>10)
     .forEach(x->sout(x));

 4. Easier Parallel Processing
 With streams + lambda, Java can easily run operations in parallel.
 Example:
 list.parallelStream()
     .filter(x -> x > 10)
     .forEach(System.out::println);
 -This helps with multi-core processing.

 what is Lambda Expression:
 -It is an Anonymous function
 -nameless
 -without return type
 -without modifiers
- it is a short way to implement a functional interface using an anonymous function
syntax:  (parameters)->expession
example: (a,b)-> a+b

4️⃣ Uses of Lambda Expressions in Java:
To reduce the amount of code (write shorter code).
To implement functional interfaces easily.
To iterate through collections like List, Set, and Map.
To filter data from collections.
To sort collections easily.
To work with the Stream API for data processing.
To perform parallel processing for better performance.
To make code more readable and cleaner.
To replace anonymous classes in many cases.
To write functional-style programming in Java.


Steps to Convert to Lambda Expression:
1️⃣ Start with Anonymous Class (Old Way)
Comparator<Integer> c = new Comparator<Integer>() {
    public int compare(Integer a, Integer b) {
        return a - b;
    }
};

2️⃣ Remove Class Declaration
Remove:

new Comparator<Integer>()
{ } class block
Now we only focus on the method part.
3️⃣ Remove Method Name and Modifier
Remove:
public
int
compare
Keep parameters and logic only.
(Integer a, Integer b) {
    return a - b;
}

4️⃣ Add Lambda Arrow ->
(Integer a, Integer b) -> {
    return a - b;
}

5️⃣ Remove Parameter Data Types (Optional)
Java can automatically detect types.

(a, b) -> {
    return a - b;
}

6️⃣ Remove Curly Braces if One Line
(a, b) -> return a - b

7️⃣ Remove return if Single Expression

Final Lambda Expression:

(a, b) -> a - b

Final Lambda Code
Comparator<Integer> c = (a, b) -> a - b;

