1️⃣ Lambda Without Curly Braces { }
When the lambda has only one expression, you do NOT use return.
The compiler automatically returns the value.
Example
interface Add {
    int sum(int a, int b);
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Lambda without curly braces
        Add obj = (a, b) -> a + b;

        System.out.println(obj.sum(10, 20));
    }
}

Explanation
(a, b) -> a + b


Here:
No { }
No return
Compiler automatically returns a + b
✔ Output
30
______________________________________________________
2️⃣ Lambda With Curly Braces { }

When you use curly braces, the lambda body behaves like a normal method block.
So return becomes compulsory if the method returns a value.

Example
interface Add {
    int sum(int a, int b);
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Lambda with curly braces
        Add obj = (a, b) -> {
            return a + b;
        };
        System.out.println(obj.sum(10, 20));
    }
}

Explanation
(a, b) -> {
    return a + b;
}


Here:
{ } used
So return statement is required
✔ Output
30
